WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.118
Safe process execution means launching external programs from Python only
when there is a clear reason, and doing it in a way that is constrained,
reviewable, and recoverable.

2
00:00:10.118 --> 00:00:23.449
Python applications often start child processes from web applications,
APIs, command-line tools, automation scripts, data pipelines, background
workers, scheduled jobs, build tools, and internal administrative
utilities.

3
00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:30.491
The pattern is powerful, but it reaches outside normal Python control and
asks the operating system to run something else.

4
00:00:30.491 --> 00:00:38.968
Process execution is different from calling a Python library, using a
system API, or scheduling a background job inside an application
framework.

5
00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:44.984
A library call usually stays inside the process and follows the runtime
permissions of the application.

6
00:00:44.984 --> 00:00:56.264
A child process may interpret arguments differently, inherit environment
variables, read and write files, use network access, produce output, or
continue running after the parent has moved on.

7
00:00:56.264 --> 00:01:00.845
Those differences create a security boundary that deserves design
attention.

8
00:01:00.845 --> 00:01:05.562
The risks affect confidentiality, integrity, availability, and
auditability.

9
00:01:05.562 --> 00:01:18.551
A child process may receive secrets it does not need, read files outside
the intended workspace, write output to an unsafe location, run too long,
consume too many resources, or expose internal details through logs and
errors.

10
00:01:18.551 --> 00:01:28.806
If untrusted input influences command structure, file paths, environment
variables, or options, the application can become an unsafe interface to
operating system behavior.

11
00:01:28.806 --> 00:01:37.215
The safest design is often to avoid external process execution entirely
when a trusted library or platform feature can perform the work directly.

12
00:01:37.215 --> 00:01:59.160
When a process is genuinely needed, the design should be explicit: choose
the executable from trusted configuration, pass arguments as structured
values, avoid shell interpretation, validate inputs for the approved
operation, control the environment, limit privileges, set timeouts, handle
output carefully, and log enough to investigate without exposing sensitive
data.
